In the Riverside district, for instance, a green corridor project cleaned up the river, added cycle paths, and planted thousands of trees. On paper, it seems like a clear environmental success. Yet many long‑term residents have a different view. As the area became more attractive, property prices and rents rose, small local shops struggled, and some original tenants could no longer afford to stay.

The problem is not urban greening itself; cleaner air and more public spaces are valuable. The issue is that environmental goals are often pursued without considering social impacts. When that happens, projects presented as helping everyone can quietly make life harder for the very communities they were meant to support.', '{"file":"20260701_07.mp3","genre":"A loosely structured speech","skill":"Identifying key messages","audioKind":"a short talk"}', 1751860800000);

One issue is how productivity is measured. Many organizations still equate visible activity—emails, meetings, constant online presence—with meaningful output. In that context, remote staff may feel obliged to prove they are “present,” even if this interrupts their concentration. So when surveys claim that remote workers are “more productive,” it is not always clear whether they are truly achieving more, or just doing more things that look like work.

Outcomes also depend heavily on organizational design. Companies that rethink goals, information‑sharing, and performance criteria often find remote or hybrid work can function well, whereas those that simply copy office routines into digital tools may amplify existing problems. Asking whether remote work is generally “good” or “bad” for productivity is therefore less useful than asking under what conditions, and for whom, it actually works.', '{"file":"20260701_08.mp3","genre":"A loosely structured speech","skill":"Inferring attitudes","audioKind":"a short talk"}', 1751860800000);

But let’s be honest for a moment. When I hear colleagues say they’ll ‘just tweak’ their existing projects to fit the latest funding buzzwords, I can’t exactly blame them. The guidelines are vague, the targets keep moving, and half the time it feels like we’re jumping through hoops just to prove we’re not irrelevant. So yes, engagement matters—but if we’re serious about it, we need something better than a box‑ticking exercise wrapped in glossy language.', '{"file":"20260701_12.mp3","genre":"Short talk with shift from formal to informal register","skill":"Inferring stance and identifying tension between official rhetoric and lived experience","audioKind":"a short talk"}', 1751860800000);

Off the record, though, the tone is rather different. Senior managers talk about ‘not scaring investors,’ ‘keeping the numbers pretty,’ and ‘getting the campaign out the door’ before anyone asks awkward questions about long‑term commitments. I’m not saying nothing has changed—there are genuine efforts—but when the cameras stop rolling, it’s hard to shake the feeling that image still comes first, and substance is expected to catch up later.', '{"file":"20260701_13.mp3","genre":"Short talk with shift from formal to informal register","skill":"Inferring nuanced criticism","audioKind":"a short talk"}', 1751860800000);
B (man’s voice): “Oh, it went exactly as planned… if the plan was to confuse everyone and crash the system twice before lunch.”
A: “That bad?”
B: “Let’s just say it takes a special kind of talent to send three different ‘final’ versions of the same update in one morning.”
A: “Ouch. I guess the IT team’s not having a great day.”
B: “To be fair, they’ve been under a lot of pressure, and I don’t think anyone set out to make our lives harder. But if we’re going to learn anything from this, we need a clear timeline, proper testing, and maybe—just maybe—fewer surprises on the day we go live.”', '{"file":"20260701_14.mp3","genre":"Conversation — irony & humor","skill":"Recognising the irony plus the shift from sarcasm to constructive suggestions","audioKind":"a short conversation"}', 1751860800000);
Student 1: “Did it work?”
Lecturer: “Not even slightly. I’ve learned that no amount of colourful diagrams can save an explanation that doesn’t actually make sense.”
Student 2: “We do sometimes just nod along when we’re lost…”
Lecturer: “Exactly. Students are remarkably good at nodding politely while having absolutely no idea what I’m talking about. So if something I say today sounds mysterious, please stop me before it turns into one of those ‘nodding but not understanding’ situations.”', '{"file":"20260701_15.mp3","genre":"Conversation — self-deprecating humor","skill":"Identifying the self‑deprecating humor","audioKind":"a discussion between a lecturer and two students"}', 1751860800000);
Speaker 1: “On one level, of course they matter; it would be odd to argue that personal behaviour is irrelevant.”
Speaker 2: “Right, but look what happens when we stop the conversation there. Governments can delay tough decisions, companies can carry on as usual, and we all pat ourselves on the back for carrying reusable bags while emissions keep rising.”
Moderator: “So you’re saying personal choices aren’t enough?”
Speaker 2: “I’m saying: when we emphasise personal responsibility, we should also ask who benefits when the story ends with the individual consumer. That’s the part that often gets left out.”', '{"file":"20260701_16.mp3","genre":"Moderated panel discussion","skill":"Tracking the rhetorical move and identifying the speaker’s view","audioKind":"a moderated panel discussion"}', 1751860800000);
Colleague B: “They said it was… ‘interesting.’ They ‘appreciated the ambition,’ which usually means they think we’re aiming far too high.”
Colleague A: “That doesn’t sound very promising.”
Colleague B: “They also ‘welcomed the clarity of the analysis,’ and then immediately asked for a completely new set of figures.”
Colleague A: “Did they at least give you a clear yes or no?”
Colleague B: “Not exactly. They suggested we ‘take some time to reflect’ and ‘consider a more incremental approach.’ So we’re not dead in the water, but we’re definitely not launching next month either.”', '{"file":"20260701_17.mp3","genre":"Conversation — identifying attitudes","skill":"Inferring and identifying attitudes","audioKind":"a dialogue between two colleagues after a meeting"}', 1751860800000);

That said, we need to be honest with ourselves. The analysis doesn’t yet support the strength of the claims we’re making, and some of the recommendations read more like wishful thinking than evidence‑based advice. If we send it out in its current form, it will look polished on the surface but fragile underneath, and that is not a risk I’m prepared to take.', '{"file":"20260701_18.mp3","genre":"Supervisor commenting on report quality","skill":"Recognising the shift from formal to a more blunt critical stance","audioKind":"a short talk"}', 1751860800000);

The minute the door closed, though, his style changed completely. He said, ‘We can’t keep sugar‑coating this. We’re weeks behind, and everyone’s acting like that’s just business as usual.’ His voice was sharper, and he stopped using those careful phrases. It was pretty clear who he was really frustrated with, and it wasn’t the client.', '{"file":"20260701_19.mp3","genre":"Public register to private tone","skill":"Tracking the contrast between the public, reassuring and the private, sharper tone to infer the underlying stance","audioKind":"a short talk"}', 1751860800000);

I’m a little worried, though, that the tone might come across as more combative than you intend. Phrases like ‘it is undeniable’ and ‘previous work has completely overlooked’ can sound as if you’re dismissing other researchers rather than building on them. If that’s what you want to do, fine—but if not, you might want to soften some of the language so people hear you as joining the conversation, not shutting it down.', '{"file":"20260701_20.mp3","genre":"Criticism","skill":"Recognising hedged criticism embedded in polite wording and how specific lexical choices affect interpersonal meaning and stance","audioKind":"a short talk"}', 1751860800000);


